```java
// WRONG: authentication and authorization mixed into one method
public String handle(String user, String pass, String path) {
    if (!user.equals("admin") || !pass.equals("secret")) {
        return "401"; // identity check (authentication)
    }
    if (!path.equals("/reports")) {
        return "403"; // permission check tangled into the same place
    }
    return "OK";
}
```
Mixing the two makes the rules impossible to reuse and easy to get wrong.

```java
// RIGHT: Spring Security keeps authentication and authorization separate
@Bean
SecurityFilterChain chain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    return http
        .httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults())            // authentication: who are you?
        .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth             // authorization: what can you do?
            .requestMatchers("/reports").hasRole("ADMIN")
            .anyRequest().authenticated())
        .build();
}
```
Identity is verified once, then each request is authorized against declarative rules.
